---
import Card from "./Card.astro";
import Label from "./Label.astro";
---

<section class="border-b border-neutral-950 py-20 sm:py-28">
	<Label>05 / Smarter updates</Label>
	<h2
		class="mt-6 max-w-3xl text-4xl font-bold leading-tight tracking-tight sm:text-5xl"
	>
		Updates that arrive pre-triaged.
	</h2>
	<p class="mt-6 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-neutral-700">
		Paranoia decides what is safe to update. Next, uppy uses AI to make the safe
		updates easier to act on — without ever taking the human out of the loop.
	</p>

	<div class="mt-12 grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-3">
		<Card kicker="Summaries" title="Knows what changed" upcoming>
			<p>
				AI distills the changelog, release notes, and commits into a short,
				honest summary of what an update actually does — so you are not reading
				upstream history yourself.
			</p>
		</Card>

		<Card kicker="Relevance" title="Sorted by what matters to you" upcoming>
			<p>
				AI reads what your project <em>is</em> and ranks the queue accordingly.
				In an Astro-on-Cloudflare app, an
				<code class="font-mono text-neutral-950">astro</code>
				or Cloudflare bump outranks a dev-only CLI — urgent and central first,
				trivial last.
			</p>
		</Card>

		<Card kicker="Codemods" title="Fixes breaks before you look" upcoming>
			<p>
				When an update would break your code, AI attempts the fix on the PR
				branch so it builds — and tells you what it could not. A human always
				reviews and merges; uppy never merges on its own.
			</p>
		</Card>
	</div>
</section>
